      "math_academy_id": "q-130737",
      "sequence_position": 14,
      "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(C)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(-2C)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-2C) & =(-2)^{4}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-80.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
